<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00Y7">Notation <span data-tag="00Y7">3.3.3.2</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a simplicial set. It follows immediately from the definitions that if there exists a simplicial set $Y$ and a morphism $u: X \rightarrow \operatorname{Ex}(Y)$ which exhibits $Y$ as a subdivision of $X$, then the simplicial set $Y$ (and the morphism $u$) are uniquely determined up to isomorphism and depend functorially on $X$. To emphasize this dependence, we will denote $Y$ by $\operatorname{Sd}(X)$ and refer to it as <em>the</em> subdivision of $X$. </p>
